And he was not from Idstein, but everybody knew this guy. And they didn't like him, for whatever reason. He was just not a likable person. You know. And he was a-- a big shot in the Nazi party, as I later learned. I-- I think I even remember his name. I'm not positive.

INT: What was it?

RL: I think his name was Willie-- it was short. Ott, O-T-T. Ott. Willie Ott. Short guy. Round face. Blond hair. Middle-aged. Well, I mean, to me, eight years old, you know. 20-year-old is middle-aged.

But anyway, he wanted to buy that business for a long time. My father always refused, because he didn't want the neighbors to have that kind of a-- a person, because they didn't like him. So one day, he came to my mother. And he said, I know where your father is. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Where's my husband? Or not my-- my father, her husband. Where is my husband? He's in Buchenwald. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So I'll get him out for you if you sign these papers. OK?

So in effect, you know, I mean, whether it was true or not, whether it was capable of being done or not, that was a no-brainer for my mother, where do I sign. You know, so you-- you know, I say that now, but it obviously wasn't that easy a decision.

But the final thing that happened was my mother did sign some sort of a document which allowed him to take over the warehouse and the offices and stay there. It belonged to him. And at the same time, we would sell the house to him, with the understanding that the family could remain there until they were able to leave, for one mark. OK? I suppose to make it legal.

INT: What would that be the equivalent of in-- in--

RL: $1. OK? One Deutsche Mark. So my mother then, you know, took care of that. And we waited. And we waited. And sure enough, one fine day in March of 1939, my mother gets a phone call. It's my father. I'm out. I'm out.

And he was in Buchenwald all that time. And he did manage to get him out. And [SIGHS] I remember my mother instructing me-- well, evidently, my father instructed her to gather as many clothes and as much money as she could and get it to him. And I believe it was my obligation, being the little kid and who could get around in all the nooks and alleys after dark to meet my father at a predetermined place near the railroad station and to give him this. And she says, don't get lost, and don't get caught.

So it was quite dark. It was quite late. But I went. I told you, I do what I'm told. And indeed, I did meet my father. I didn't recognize him. He was gone from November to March, you know, just a few months, and he lost maybe 50, 60 pounds.

He had no hair. You know, they-- the-- the J cut into his hair, the crew cut type stuff. And I don't-- I don't remember what he was wearing. Well, we just cried and hugged and hugged and cried for as long as we were able to.

INT: You were how old?

RL: I was at that-- nine-- nine years old. 1939. And-- and I gave him the sack of goodies, and I found my way home. Didn't get caught. And that's the last we heard of him for a while. And he-- as we now know-- made his way from Germany to Holland to England and subsequently to the United States. This was in 1939. And once he got here, he got the ball rolling to get us out.

And in those days, it was still legal. They didn't have the camps yet. OK? But even the Jewish families that hadn't-- that were there during Kristallnacht had eventually-- some of them were taken to concentration camps.

Some of them emigrated to other cities. Some committed suicide. I remember, two people committed suicide. And we were the only Jewish family left at this point in time in Germany, still waiting for a legal way to get out.

INT: In-- in your town?

RL: In my town. In Idstein. And the time came, time passed, and the time came when we got the-- the-- the information that yes, we got permission to get out of Germany. And you know, exciting times.

So at that point in time, and I think it was around [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] March, April, in that general time, when we got the information, my mother took all of our belongings that we had at that point, all our clothes, whatever we had-- and by the way, I should say that no German, Jewish or otherwise, could not leave the country with more than 100 marks per person. $100, if you want to put it in our language. And that's fine.

So my mother got these-- well, they were called-- they were called lifts in German, which-- which are really crates, huge crates, OK, wooden crates. And every-- everything that we owned was put into those crates. And I remember my mother sitting upstairs in her sewing room. And for days, for days, was taking all the clothes and sewing whatever money she could find and sewing it into the clothes. Everything we had went into those clothes.

And every time I opened the door to go in her room, she chased me out. Nobody was supposed to see all this, but I remember it. And so finally, the day came when all the sewing was finished, and all the clothes and whatever belongings we had were put into these huge crates. And I remember a truck backing up to the side of the house, and we couldn't get out the front door. They had to take it out to the courtyard, in the back, big door.

And they put these crates-- I don't remember how many there were, two or three, including suitcases of all kinds-- on these crates. So everything we-- literally, we owned was on its way to a freighter which was to leave for the United States from Rotterdam. So the truck went to bring it to the freighter in Rotterdam. And indeed, it did get onto the freighter. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

On the morning that the ship was supposed to leave, the Nazi Air Force decided to bomb Rotterdam Harbor. OK? And all our belongings, whatever we had on those crates, is now on the bottom of the sea, on this freighter. And there it still remains.

INT: What year was this? What year would this be?

RL: I'm sorry?

INT: What year would this be?

RL: This is 1939. This is in-- about March, April 1939. I'm not sure about the dates. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And so that was that. You know. And so then we got the dates on which we're allowed to leave. And we're now going. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] The day has come for us to leave.

