RL: When-- when I was no longer able to go to school, that kindergarten incident, my sisters weren't either. And they went to school, the only Jewish school that was still open at that time. I'm going back now to 1936, '37, in Wiesbaden. And they went to school in Wiesbaden. They had to take a train back and forth, go to Wiesbaden instead of--

INT: How close were they to each other?

RL: I-- I would imagine about a half hour, 45 minute ride by train. And that's what sticks in my mind. I'm not quite sure. But they had to go to Idstein-- and-- and to-- to Wiesbaden, to the Jewish school.

And I remember later on, after I couldn't go to-- to school in Idstein, when I got a little older, it must have been around 1937 or so, '36, '37, where I too, for a short period of time, went to the same school in Idstein with them. And it's just some funny stories that-- that happened that, you know, I don't know if you're interested, but I'll-- I'll tell you anyway.

[LAUGHTER]

With my-- in those days, again, we had the problem with the food. And they did have some stores that would sell to Jewish trade. There may be even Jewish stores for all I know. There were a handful in certain pockets in the big cities.

And I recall that certain days of the week, my mother instructed my oldest sister to buy certain produce and bring it back from Wiesbaden. So one day, I remember, we were going to this butcher store. And she bought these-- I guess we call them frankfurters. I know in Germany they were [NON-ENGLISH], I guess you call them. They were attached by string. You know, they were all attached. You could pull them like that.

And she had a big brown bag full. And the line was kind of long, and we were late. Because we've got to catch the train. So we finally got out of there. And it was raining cats and dogs.

So we ran to the railroad station. And she had the books in one hand and the bag in the other. And she ran, and she ran, and the rain came down, and the bag got all soggy. And the hot dogs started to come out the bottom, one by one. And all of a sudden, we had a following of these one or two dogs.

And they were trailing my sister's hot dogs and ate the hot dogs, all the way until they couldn't anymore. And so when we got home, we didn't have all the hot dogs that we were supposed to have. But that-- I-- I-- I laughed, and I just thought it was the funniest story. But funny for me, maybe not funny for anybody else.

So that was-- that was that story. And I also remember during the time when we didn't know where my father was, and we got fed from Mrs. Meyer as much as we could, my mother one day was very frustrated about something my sisters did. I don't-- I don't even know which sister.

But anyway, she was very frustrated, and she was angry about something. And she threw an egg at them, a raw egg, she threw at my sister, and it broke. And everybody stopped short. I mean, this is a, you know, a week's supply of food. What are you doing? And that's how frustrated she was about something, you know. But that sticks in my mind as a memory.

INT: Did your father tell you anything about his experiences, Buchenwald?

RL: Well, not really. I don't-- you know. He didn't-- I guess he didn't want to-- that part of his life, which was not a positive thing. He didn't want to give to his children. Of course, we knew what happened, you know. And later on, we found out all the things.

But he was just one of the fortunate people who was able to get out. You know. Obviously for some reason, but you know, there-- there were not many people who got out at that point in time for whatever reason. But he was one of the fortunate ones.

INT: Was Mr. Ott instrumental in getting him out?

RL: Yeah. He was the-- you know, because we signed that over. That's why he got out. So yes, indeed. That was the reason.

INT: Was Mr. Ott a-- a Nazi?

RL: Oh, yeah. Oh, was he ever.

INT: Was in the party?

RL: But he wanted that house so badly, he found any means he could to try and get that house. Not only did he get the warehouse and the office he wanted, he got the house he wanted. And he got it for nothing. You know. But at-- he didn't get it for nothing. He got it for the most valuable thing that we had, sure, obviously.

INT: Did you know what happened after he took over the properties?

RL: He did allow us to live there. I do know I used to roam around at-- at will, as I told you, in-- into the warehouse. And he would chase me. Every time I go to a door I wasn't supposed to be anymore, I would, you know, I would be chased out.

And he would yell at the top of his lung, you know, and not only to me, but to anybody who went into his 'territory.' But, you know, this was a minor thing as far as we were concerned. We were just waiting for the day to get out, and that was it. You know, we just bided our time.

INT: What kind of business did he put in the warehouse?

RL: I have no idea. I really don't know. I know he ran a business from there. I don't know.

INT: What happened to the feed and grain business that your father had?

RL: Well, that just went, because there were no more people who would sell to my father. OK? And there were no more-- there was no more product that he could sell to his customers, eventually. So it just-- a natural course, you know. It just died.

INT: Getting back to Kristallnacht, you were telling about, of course, the trauma of what had happened. After that, did you say anything to these people that you thought were friends of yours?

RL: Well, I had no contact with any of them. OK? And I don't think my parents, at that point in time, wanted any contact with them. But I-- I do remember there were a handful of people like Mrs. Meyer. Mrs. Meyer was always the one who didn't care what happened to her.

Let them talk. Let them, you know, ostracize me. Let them kill me. She's in her 80s, getting on. You know, what are they going to do her? I think that was her-- her attitude.

But she's the one I remember the most. I know there were other people, neighbors across the street, who although they-- they weren't part of this physically, by just condoning it, they were part of it. OK?

INT: Do you remember their names?

RL: No. Faces, maybe. You know. I-- I-- names have just gone away. Can't remember their names. But I remember there were several like that who-- who, you know, were friendly always with my parents, but after Kristallnacht couldn't or wouldn't keep contact. And so, you know, so we lived by ourselves. We were in our own little ghetto within our house.

INT: So you did-- at that point, you didn't have any relationship with the neighbors, or did you?

RL: Not that I recall. Because I was always told, stay in the house. The only time I was out of the house is when I went to the railroad station to go to school and come back. I mean, it's such a small town, everybody knew each other, but they all knew that they had to, you know, not-- not have relationships of any kind with Jewish people.

And there weren't that many Jewish people left. You know, as I said, at the time we left, in 1940, we were the only ones left, and I think Kristallnacht, there may have been only about four or five or six families at that point-- not much more.

INT: Well, did you have any problems going to school in Wiesbaden?

RL: It was short-lived, because after a while, they just closed down all schools, including the Jewish school. And problems, I-- I don't-- I wouldn't call it problems, just I do remember there was a period of over a year where I didn't go to school at all. Because once they didn't allow us to go to school in Wiesbaden anymore, I just didn't go to school until I, you know, came to this country.

INT: Were you able to be taught by anyone else in this period when you weren't going to school?

RL: Well, we did have-- the rabbi was still one of the Jewish families. I think his name was [NON-ENGLISH] Hess, Joseph Hess, H-E-S, I think. And he, when we would get together for any reason, you know, and it was rare, he would always have a little lesson for the kids.

He would just-- whether it was a Jewish lesson, or whether it was just anything else, you know. But I remember, we got together and-- and just talked. And whether that was education, I suppose it was, but it wasn't any formal education of any kind that I recall during that approximate one-year span from 1939 to '40.

INT: Now at this point, was the synagogue burned down?

RL: Oh, it was burned down. It was burned down, yes.

INT: And was there any other houses of worship around?

RL: No. This was the only synagogue in town for the, at one point, 18 Jewish families, which was enough. I mean, one synagogue was enough, so that no, there was no need for any other.

INT: Did the Jewish people try to meet at all other Jewish families?

RL: Well, again, there weren't that many left. And yes, those that were there obviously clung to each other. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] But I have no memory of, you know, who they were and when they left and under what-- under what circumstances they left. I only have memory of my own.

INT: Did you keep in touch with any of the families other than you had mentioned Henry, I believe?

RL: Henry.

INT: Was that his name? The-- the--

RL: Oh, [? Heinz. ?] [? Heinz. ?]

INT: Heinz, I'm sorry.

RL: Henry, oh, now I know who you mean. No. And I only met him once. It was a coincidental. I found out where he was. And at that point in time, I was a salesman. And I was in his area, so I called him up, are you home, and we met and had a very nice afternoon together. But there was never a close relationship since, you know.

INT: Other than not being allowed to go to school, what other restrictions were put on the Jews?

RL: Well, the restrictions you-- you couldn't go out after dark. You had to be indoors. You couldn't buy the foodstuffs that were rationed, certainly not to the extent that the others could. You were just ostracized. I mean, it didn't have to be written in stone. You just were ostracized, simple as that, for the-- for the mere fact that you happen to worship to a different god, I suppose.

