INT: Do you know the process that was required in order to get out of Germany?

RL: Frankly, no. The details, I don't know. And I was a child, and it was never told to me. So I-- and I didn't inquire. But I was just glad to be here. That's all, be out of there.

INT: And who immigrated with you to the States?

RL: My mother, my grandfather, and my two sisters and I.

INT: And you, personally, what was it that you were allowed to leave with?

RL: Well, each of us was allowed to take no more in-- in-- in funds, in German funds, in more than 100 marks. So I suppose, there were five of us, we had 500 marks, and whatever we could carry in our little suitcases, so you know.

INT: Do you remember what you took?

RL: No. It was just whatever clothes. There wasn't much left after-- after my mother packed the crates, which are now, you know, in the bottom of the sea. But we each had a little-- a little suitcase to carry. And I carried mine, just like they carried theirs, and that was that.

INT: Now, do you remember the day, actually, of your leaving?

RL: Oh yeah.

INT: What was that like?

RL: May 1, 1940. That was the day when we left. And that was the day when I told you we saw that newspaper article with Idstein Judenfrei. And that was the day we-- we spent-- I think it was an overnight trip on the train. I think I slept on a sleeper for the first time in a big train.

